        <div id="who-discounts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Protein Powder Brands and Stores Usually Discount on Black Friday?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The deepest protein discounts usually come from brands that sell mainly through their own websites and from the biggest name brands at large retailers. We cannot tell you this year's offers until they are posted, so treat what follows as patterns from past years, not promises.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Direct-to-consumer brands</h3>

          <p>
            Brands that sell mostly on their own sites, such as Transparent Labs, Myprotein, Legion and Naked Nutrition, have typically run sitewide percentage-off codes over Black Friday weekend. Some, Myprotein in particular, run discounts so often that their list price is rarely what anyone pays, so judge them only on the final price per 25 g at checkout, after the code, not on the percentage off.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Big name brands at big retailers</h3>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition, Dymatize, Isopure, Premier Protein, Muscle Milk and Garden of Life are stocked by Amazon, Target, Walmart and the supplement chains, so they are often discounted somewhere. Because the same product is sold in many places, these are also the easiest tubs to price-check: if one store's deal is weak, another's may be better. Our <a href="/best-target-protein-powder/">Target</a>, <a href="/best-walmart-protein-powder/">Walmart</a> and <a href="/best-costco-protein-powder/">Costco</a> guides list each store's normal prices.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Supplement chains</h3>

          <p>
            The Vitamin Shoppe and GNC lean on multi-buy offers such as Buy One, Get One 50% Off, member pricing and their own brands. A BOGO 50% offer on two tubs of the same price works out to 25 percent off each, which is a real deal on a powder that was fairly priced to begin with and not on one marked up in store. Our <a href="/best-vitamin-shoppe-protein-powder/">Vitamin Shoppe protein guide</a> gives the normal prices of its BodyTech and True Athlete powders, and the 15 percent Auto Delivery discount the store already offers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Store brands</h3>

          <p>
            House brands such as Kirkland at Costco, Elevation at Aldi, Equate at Walmart and Meijer's own whey are rarely discounted much, because they are already priced low. They make a useful benchmark: if a name-brand Black Friday deal does not beat the store brand on the same shelf per 25 g, it is not much of a deal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fake-deals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Spot a Fake Black Friday Protein Deal?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common fake deal is a big percentage off a list price that was raised before the sale. A 40 percent discount from an inflated price can land on the normal price, or above it. The fix is to ignore the percentage and the strike-through and judge the price per 25 g against the table above or against a price-history chart.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price-history tools such as Keepa and camelcamelcamel show what an Amazon listing has cost over months. If the Black Friday price is the same as the price two months ago, it is not a Black Friday deal. For other stores, the normal prices in our store guides and in the product cards on this page are a reasonable baseline.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for shrinking tubs too. A sale on a smaller size, or a new size with fewer servings than the one you usually buy, can look cheap per tub and cost more per gram. Read the serving count and the protein per serving before you compare. The same goes for bundles: a tub plus a shaker and a sample pack is only a deal if the tub alone is at a good price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, check who is selling it. On Amazon, a very low price from an unfamiliar third-party seller can mean old stock near its best-by date, a grey-market import or worse. Buy from the brand, from Amazon itself, or from a large retailer, and check the best-by date when the tub arrives.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stock-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Much Protein Powder Should You Buy on Black Friday?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy as much as you will use before the best-by date, and no more than you have room to store somewhere cool and dry. For most people that is three to six months of supply. At one 25 g shake a day, a 5 lb tub with 70 to 80 servings lasts about two and a half months, so two big tubs is a sensible Black Friday haul.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealed protein powder typically carries a best-by date 18 to 24 months after it is made, and the tub you buy has already used some of that. Whey kept sealed in a cool cupboard keeps well, but flavors can fade and the powder can clump if it gets damp. Our guide to <a href="/does-protein-powder-expire/">whether protein powder expires</a> covers how to store it and what to look for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Only stock up on a powder and flavor you already know you like. A 30 percent discount is not worth much on a 5 lb tub you cannot finish. If you want to try something new on Black Friday, buy the smallest size or a sample pack, even though it is the worst value per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            The math of stocking up is simple. If you use one shake a day and save 50 cents per 25 g, that is about $15 a month, or $45 to $90 on a three- to six-month supply. It is worth an evening of price-checking. It is not worth buying a powder you would not otherwise use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Protein Powder Deals Should You Skip on Black Friday?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip discounted mass gainers unless you need the calories, small premium bags with a big percentage off, and any powder whose sale price is still above the normal median for its type. Those are the deals that feel good and save little.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mass gainers are the classic trap. A 6 lb bag of Serious Mass costs $57.88 on Amazon in our database, which sounds cheap until you see it holds 8 servings and works out to $3.62 per 25 g of protein. Even at 30 percent off it costs about $2.53 per 25 g, more than many full-price wheys. If you need extra calories, add milk, oats and peanut butter to a cheap whey instead; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">weight gain protein guide</a> shows how.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premium bags of 15 to 30 servings with superfoods, greens or collagen often cost $3 to $6 per 25 g at full price. Half off still leaves many of them above the median whey. Buy them if you want the extras, but do not buy them as a bargain. The same applies to meal shakes such as Ka'Chava, which cost $5.50 per 25 g of protein at the Vitacost price in our database.
          </p>

          <p>
            And skip anything you have not checked the label on. A Black Friday deal on a powder with 15 g of protein per scoop, a proprietary blend or a sweetener you dislike is not a deal at all. Our <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/">best cheap protein powder</a> rankings list the powders that are good value even at full price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What If You Miss the Black Friday Protein Deals?
          </h2>

          <p>
            You have not missed much. Protein powder goes on sale several times a year: Amazon's July and October deal events, January new-year sales, and brand anniversary and holiday sales all bring prices close to Black Friday levels. The thresholds on this page work for any of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the meantime, the cheapest way to buy protein without a sale is to buy the biggest tub of a powder you like, use a store brand where one exists, and use a subscription discount where it does not push the price up. Several powders, including Muscle Milk Pro Series and plain pea from NOW and Naked, cost less per 25 g at their normal price than most wheys do on Black Friday.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are choosing a brand to buy on sale, our reviews check each label and testing claim: the <a href="/now-sports-protein-powder-review/">NOW Sports protein review</a> covers one of the cheapest tested proteins, and our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition</a> and <a href="/isopure-protein-powder-review/">Isopure</a> reviews cover two of the brands most often discounted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Health note: protein powder is a food supplement, not a medicine. People with kidney disease, who are pregnant or who have a milk, soy or egg allergy should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before stocking up, whatever the price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging a deal by the percentage off</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 40 percent discount from an inflated list price can land on the normal price. Ignore the percentage and the strike-through. Work out the sale price per 25 g of protein and compare it with the normal price for that type; if it is not at least 25 percent lower, it is not a Black Friday deal.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing different tub sizes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Retailers discount different sizes, and a cheap small tub can cost more per gram than a full-price big one. Always divide by servings and protein per serving. A 2 lb tub at 30 percent off can still lose to a 5 lb tub at 15 percent off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stocking up on an untried flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Five pounds of a flavor you dislike is the most expensive protein you will ever buy. Stock up only on powders and flavors you already use. If you want to try something new, buy the smallest size, even though it costs more per gram.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a mass gainer as cheap protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gainers look cheap per pound because most of each pound is carbohydrate. Serious Mass costs about $3.62 per 25 g of protein at full price and about $2.53 even at 30 percent off, more than most whey at full price. Buy a gainer for calories, never for protein value.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the seller and best-by date</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unusually low prices from unknown third-party sellers can mean old stock, imports or counterfeits. Buy from the brand, Amazon itself or a big retailer, check the best-by date on arrival, and do not buy more than you will finish before it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

